WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.670
My class trained a model to

2
00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.930
understand spoken numbers, and

3
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.510
it scored ninety per cent.

4
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:07.900
We cheered!

5
00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.050
Then we looked closer: most of its

6
00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:13.050
mistakes were on girls’ voices.

7
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.220
Today: how to make your model

8
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:18.200
fair for everyone it judges.

9
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.380
One overall score can hide a problem.

10
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.870
Most recordings were boys’ voices, and the

11
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:27.690
model got almost all of those right.

12
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.350
On girls’ voices, it got

13
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.710
about seven in ten.

14
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.140
Mixed together, that still

15
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.520
looked like ninety per cent.

16
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.480
So test each group on its own.

17
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:40.740
Split your test examples by

18
00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.560
group: boys and girls, young and

19
00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.800
old, one accent and another.

20
00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.000
Count right and wrong for each.

21
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.900
If one group scores much lower,

22
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.820
your model is failing them.

23
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.540
When a model makes more mistakes for one

24
00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:58.500
group than for others, it’s biased.

25
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.990
Bias usually starts in the data: a

26
00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:04.030
group that’s missing, or too small.

27
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.160
In Mr Joseph’s class, three in

28
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:08.940
four recordings were boys’.

29
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.190
It’s like a tailor who learned from

30
00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:13.590
one person’s measurements.

31
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:16.500
Every shirt fits that person perfectly.

32
00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.900
On everyone else, the

33
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:19.920
sleeves are too short!

34
00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.310
The tailor isn’t unkind; they

35
00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:24.710
never measured anyone else.

36
00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:26.590
Quick check!

37
00:01:27.380 --> 00:01:29.620
A leaf-disease model scores

38
00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.440
ninety-five per cent overall.

39
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.030
What should you check next?

40
00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:37.870
Its score for each region, or

41
00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.010
each group it serves.

42
00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.890
A great overall score can hide a

43
00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.390
group the model fails badly.

44
00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.790
So test groups separately, every time.

45
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.450
So how do you fix bias?

46
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.140
Go back to the data.

47
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.330
Collect examples from the missing group,

48
00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.090
with permission, and label them carefully.

49
00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:01.670
Then train again and test every group.

50
00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.240
When the girls in Mr Joseph’s class

51
00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.820
recorded their numbers, the gap almost

52
00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:07.500
closed.

53
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.130
Why does this matter?

54
00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.460
Because models judge real people.

55
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:15.950
A leaf spotter that fails hill

56
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.810
farmers can cost them a harvest.

57
00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.710
A voice tool that can’t hear an

58
00:02:20.710 --> 00:02:22.290
accent shuts people out.

59
00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.440
Fair means it works for everyone it

60
00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.540
judges, not just those in its data.

61
00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:31.030
In the app, a leaf-disease spotter

62
00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:33.430
learned mostly from sunny-valley photos,

63
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:35.750
but hill photos come out darker.

64
00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.450
Its overall score looks great,

65
00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:40.930
yet it misses about a third of

66
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:41.970
sick leaves in the hills.

67
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:45.700
Add hill photos, and watch the gap close.

68
00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:47.790
Your turn.

69
00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:50.350
Your voice model struggles with

70
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:51.450
older people’s voices.

71
00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.570
What’s the best fix?

72
00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.240
Record more older voices, with

73
00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.040
permission, then test every group again.

74
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.750
Remember: one overall score can

75
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:05.570
hide a group the model fails.

76
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:07.700
Test each group separately.

77
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.840
Fix bias at its source, the data, so

78
00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.480
the model works for everyone.

79
00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.950
Try it yourself: test voice typing or a

80
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.350
translator with your family: young and

81
00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.710
old, different accents or languages.

82
00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:26.090
Ask first, then have each person

83
00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.850
say the same three sentences.

84
00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:30.170
Count the words it gets right.

85
00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:33.310
Who does it understand best, and why?
